From bfb212412ee600ad752b9cbb78beb374217bc414 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: halt95 <halt95@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 02:49:51 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 94/97] mamba: key the prompt tail when it lands on a block end

With a hash unit finer than the block, the prompt's tail key lands
exactly on a block end once every block/unit turns. The tail key is the
position the next turn resumes at: the last unit boundary, one unit
lower under the eagle drop. It is then a full-block key, so the
partial-tail registration does nothing, and under retention interval 0
the mask kept only the replay boundary one block below it. That state
was never hashed. The next turn therefore resumed one block lower after
a cold turn, or one turn lower (at the held source) after a partial-tail
resume, re-prefilling the previous turn's extension.

MambaManager.cache_blocks now adds that block-aligned tail to the
reachable boundaries, so the block ending there is hashed like any
boundary state, retired into the held checkpoint and freed last. At the
block unit the tail is the replay boundary itself and nothing changes.
When the hash unit is below the Mamba block, MambaManager logs one
startup line saying the block-aligned tail key is hashed.

Tests: a cold and a partial-tail-resume case whose tail lands on a block
end, under a concurrent burst, with the no-leak check. Both fail on the
previous commit (the tail state is not keyed and the next turn resumes
lower). A block-unit case asserts the hashed set is exactly the replay
boundary.
